Here is a Java solution to the problem using logarithms. This solution does not use loops or recursion.

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // Base cases
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }

        // Use logarithm to check if n is a power of 3
        double logValue = Math.log(n) / Math.log(3);
        return logValue == (int) logValue;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution solution = new Solution();

        // Test cases
        System.out.println(solution.isPowerOfThree(27));  // true
        System.out.println(solution.isPowerOfThree(0));   // false
        System.out.println(solution.isPowerOfThree(-1));  // false
    }
}
```

This solution works by using the property of logarithms that `log(a^b) = b * log(a)`. If `n` is a power of 3, then `log(n) / log(3)` will be an integer. We use this property to check if `n` is a power of 3. If `logValue` is equal to its integer value, then `n` is a power of 3.

